== The Eighth Sermon

#quote[He will swallow up death in victory, and the Lord #smallcaps[God] will wipe away tears from all faces, and the rebukes of His people He will take away from all the earth; for the #smallcaps[Lord] has spoken it. And it will be said in that day, “Behold, this is our God; we have waited for Him, and He will save us. This is the #smallcaps[Lord]; we have waited for Him; we will be glad and rejoice in His salvation.”—Isa 25:8, 9.]

To come closer to the particulars. “It will be said in that day, ‘Behold, this is our God.’” The mouth of the Lord has spoken gracious things before; it has promised a feast, and an excellent feast. God’s way is first of all to give promises to his church. Why? His goodness comes from his goodness: his goodness of grace comes from his goodness of nature. “He is good and does good.” Now the same goodness of disposition which we call bounty, which reserves heaven and happiness for us in another world, the same goodness will not allow us to be without all comfort in this world, because the knowledge and revelation of the glory to come has much comfort in it. Therefore in mercy he not only intends the performance of glory, but out of the same fountain of goodness he intends to reveal whatever is good for his church on the way to glory. So promises of good come from the same goodness of God by which he intends heaven. For what moved God to come out of that hidden light, which no man can come into, and reveal himself in his Son? And the word in his promises, to reveal his mind to mankind, and to make known what he wants us to do, and what he will do for us? Only his goodness is the cause of all. And therefore the end of promises, in God’s intention, is to comfort us on the way to heaven, that we may have something to support us. They are promissa, quasi praemissa. They are promises and premises, sent before the thing itself.

Now this is why the glory to come is termed the joy of heaven and the glorious state to come. “You#super[+] need patience, so that you may obtain the promises.” Heaven and happiness are called the promises, because we have them assured in promises. The blessings of the New Testament are called promises: so we read of the children of the promise, indeed, the heirs of glory. Because all is conveyed by a promise, therefore all happiness is conveyed by a promise.

Now the promises are of good things. For their spring, they are free, from God’s free goodness; for their measure, full; for their truth, constant, even as God himself who promises. And therefore we may well build on them.

Use. Before I go any further, I beg you#super[+], let us count the promises of the good we have to be our best treasure, our best portion, our best riches, for they are called precious promises (2 Pet 1:4): not only because they are precious in themselves, but because they come from the precious love of God in Christ to us. They are also for precious things. They are laid hold of by precious faith, as the Scripture calls them, and therefore they are precious promises. Let us not count only the riches that we have; for what is what we have, compared to what we speak of, to what we have in promise? A Christian is rich in an inheritance yet to come to him, rich in bills and bonds. Christ has bound himself to him, and he can sue him for it when he pleases. In every kind of necessity he can sue God for good. He can go to God and say, “Remember Your promise to Your servant, Lord, in which You have caused me to trust” (Ps 119:49); and he can bind God with his own word.

But I take this only in passing, as the foundation of what I am to speak of.

From the mouth of God you#super[+] see the great promises delivered; and now we have waited for them. What answers promises is expectation and waiting.

The second thing, therefore, between the promises, in which God is a debtor, and the performance, is that there is a long time, a long day. Often God takes a long day for performing his promise: Abraham’s posterity were to be four hundred years in Egypt. And it was four thousand years from the beginning of the world till the coming of Christ, which was the promise of promises, the promise of the seed: a great long day. And therefore Christ is said to come in “the latter end of the world.” Abraham had the promise of a son, but it was not performed till he was an old man. Simeon had a promise that he would see Christ in the flesh, but he was an old man, ready to give up the ghost, before it was performed. God takes a long day for his promises; long to us, not to him, “for to him a thousand years are like a day.”

Reason 1. The promises of God are long in performing: to exercise our faith and our dependence to the full;

Reason 2. To draw us off from the creature; and

Reason 3. To endear the things promised to us, to set the greater price on them when we have them. Many other reasons might be given, if I intended to enlarge on that point. A Christian has a title to heaven. As soon as he is a Christian, he is an heir to heaven. Perhaps he may live here twenty or forty years more before God takes him up to glory. Why does God defer it so long?

Reason 4. The reason is that God will fit us for heaven little by little, and will perfume us as Esther was perfumed before she might come to Ahasuerus (Esth 2:12). There were many weeks and months of perfuming. So God will sweeten and fit us for heaven and happiness. It is a holy place; God is a holy God. Christ is that Holy One; and for us to have everlasting communion with God and Christ in so holy a place requires a great preparation. And God, by deferring it so long, will mortify our affections little by little, and will have us die to all base things here in affection before we die indeed. David had a title to the kingdom as soon as ever he was anointed; but David was fitted to be an excellent king indeed by the deferring of the promise’s performance till afterward. So between our right and title and our possession of heaven there is a long time.

Our Savior Christ lived thirty-four years before he was taken up to heaven, because he was to work our salvation. And he was willing to suspend his glory for such a time, that he might do it: to suspend the glory due to him from the first moment of his conception. For by virtue of the union, glory was due to him from the first; but because he had taken on himself to be a Mediator, out of love he would suspend the glory due to him, that he might suffer. And so God, by way of conformity, will suspend the glory due to us, that we may be conformed to Christ. Though we have a right to heaven as soon as we are born, yet God will suspend its full performance, because by correction and by length of time he will subdue, little by little, what keeps us from being conformed to our head.

And can we complain of any deferring of heaven, when we are only being conformed to our glorious head, who was content to be without heaven so long?

But to go on. As there are gracious and rich promises, and they have a long time of performance to us, and “hope deferred makes the heart sick” (Prov 13:12), so God grants a spirit to fit that expectation of his, a spirit of hope and waiting. And this waiting has something perfect in it, and something imperfect. It is a mixed condition. There is good, because there is a promise; for a promise is the declaration of God’s will concerning good. But because it is a promise of a thing not performed, there is an imperfection. So there is a mixture in the promise, and a mixture in the grace. Hope and expectation and waiting is an imperfect grace. That there are glorious things is perfection of good; that we do not have them in possession, that is the imperfection. So hope is something, but it is not possession; a promise is something, but it is not the performance; a seed is something, but it is not the plant.

Thus God mixes our condition here of perfection and imperfection. He will have us in a state of imperfection, that we may not think ourselves at home in our country when we are only on our way. Therefore he will have us in a state of imperfection, that we may long homeward; yet he will have it a state of good, that we may not sink on the way.

And not only promises; for on the way to heaven God does not keep everything for heaven. He often lets in drops of comfort in the midst of misery. At some times he reveals himself more glorious and sweet than at others. There is nothing reserved for us in another world but we have a beginning, a taste, a down payment of it here, to support us till we come to the full possession of what remains. We will have full communion of saints there; we have it here, in the taste of it. We know what it is to be acquainted with those who are gracious spirits. We will have praising of God forever there. We know the sweetness of it here in the house of God, which made David desire this one thing, “to dwell in the house of the #smallcaps[Lord] … to gaze on the beauty of the #smallcaps[Lord]” (Ps 27:4). There we will have perfect peace; here we have inward peace, unspeakable and glorious, “a peace that passes understanding” (Phil 4:7), in its beginning. There we will have joy without any mixture of its contrary; here we have joy, and “an inexpressible and glorious joy” (1 Pet 1:8). There is nothing in heaven that is perfect, that is sweet, and good, and comforting, but we have a taste and down payment of it here. The Spirit will be all in all there; there is something of it in us now: more light in our understandings, more obedience in our wills, more and more love in our affections, and it is growing more and more.

And therefore not everything is kept for the time to come; we have something begun here besides promises. There are some small degrees of performance. So the state between us and heaven is a state mixed of good and imperfection.

Now God has fitted graces suitable to that condition, and that is expectation or waiting: a fit grace and a fit disposition of soul for an imperfect condition, which is afterwards to be perfected. For full enjoyment is the condition of perfect happiness, not of waiting; for waiting implies imperfection.

This waiting carries almost all graces with it. Waiting for better times in the glory to come has them to support it. It is a bearing of the soul that is supported by many graces. For, first, we wait for what we believe. We have a spirit of faith to lead to it. And then we hope before we wait, and hope is the anchor of the soul, which holds the soul steady in all the waves and miseries of the world. It is the helmet that keeps off all the blows. This hope issues from faith; for what we believe, we hope for its accomplishment.

So all graces make way for waiting, or accompany it. The graces that accompany the waiting for good things in time to come are patience, to endure all griefs between us and the full possession of heaven; then long-suffering, which is nothing else but patience lengthened, because troubles are lengthened, and the time is lengthened. So there is patience, and patience lengthened, which we call long-suffering; and then, together with patience and long-suffering, there is contentment, without murmuring at the dispensation of God: a sense in the soul that he would have it so. He who has a heart that rises because he does not have what he would have does not wait with that grace of waiting which issues from a right spring.

God reserves joy for the time to come, for our home. We should be content to have communion with God and the souls of perfect men, and not murmur, though God exercises us with many crosses here. And therefore the Scripture calls it a silence: “In silence and in hope will be your#super[+] strength” (Isa 30:15). The soul keeps silence before God in this waiting condition, and this silence quells all risings in the soul at once; as David, “My soul kept silence before the Lord” (Ps 39:2). It will still all risings of the heart, issuing from a resignation of the soul to God, to do as he will have us do. So it implies patience and long-suffering, contentment, holy silence, without murmuring and repining.

And then it implies watchfulness over ourselves till we come to the full accomplishment of the promises, that we do not carry ourselves unworthily in the meantime; that we should not spend the time of our waiting in wickedness, to fetch sorrow from the devil, and the world to comfort us, or to be beholden to Satan. This is no waiting, but murmuring and rebellion, when in crosses and discomforts we cannot be content, but must be beholden to the devil; so there must be watchfulness. And not only so, but fruitfulness in waiting. For he waits who waits in doing good, who waits in service. He waits for his master’s coming who is doing his duty all the time in a fruitful course of service and obedience; otherwise it is no waiting. Waiting is not merely a distance of time, but a filling up of that time with all gracious conduct: with obedience, and with silence, with long-suffering and contentment, and watchfulness that we take no evil course, and service, and with fruitfulness, that we may fill up the times of waiting till the performance with all the graces, that we may have communion with God.

It is another kind of grace than the world thinks. What is the reason for all the wickedness of the world, and barrenness, and voluptuousness, but that they have not learned to wait? They hear of good things, and precious things promised; but they would have present payment, they will have something in hand. As with Dives: “Child, … during your lifetime you received your good things” (Luke 16:25); they will have their good things here. And what is the reason for wickedness, but that they will have the present pleasures of sin? We must prefer the afflictions of Christ to the pleasures of sin (Heb 11:25). Now that impatience of spirit, to have the reward here, is the cause of all sin. They have no hope, no obedience, no expectation to endure the long continuance of time. Where then is patience, and hope, and contentment?

The mark of a Christian is that he is in a waiting condition, and has the grace of waiting. Others will have the pleasures of sin, their profits and contentments, or else they will crack their consciences, and sell Christ, God, heaven, and all.

A Christian, as he has excellent things above the world, so he has the grace of expectation, and all the graces that store up and maintain that expectation till the performance comes.

And therefore it is a hard thing to be a good Christian, another thing than the world takes it to be. For note, I beg you#super[+], what is between us and heaven, which we must go through if ever we would come there. Between us and heaven, the thing promised, there are many crosses to be met with, and they must be borne, and borne as a Christian should. “We must endure many hardships to enter the kingdom of God” (Acts 14:22). Besides crosses, there are scandalous offenses, enough to drive us from the profession of religion, without grace. Sometimes good men, by their failings and fallings out, fall into sin, and fall out; and that is a scandal to wicked men. Oh, they say, who would be of this religion, when they cannot agree among themselves? This is a great hindrance and stop. It is a scandal and a stumbling block in the way, not so much in themselves. We are full of offense ourselves, and catch at anything so that we may object to the best ways. There is a root of offense in the hearts of all, because men will not go to hell without a reason.

Now because we are apt to take offense, rather than be damned without a reason, it is not easy to hold out. Besides this, Satan plies it with his temptations from affliction and from scandal; he magnifies these things in the imagination. Who would be a Christian? You see what their profession of faith is. And so he makes the way the more difficult.

And then again, look at our own disposition to suffer, to hold out, to stand fixed. There is an unsettledness, which is an inborn infirmity in our natures since the fall. We love variety, we are inconstant, and cannot fix ourselves on the best things, and we are impatient of suffering anything. We are not only indisposed to do good, but more indisposed to suffer any ill. The Spirit must help us over all this, which must continue all our life long. Till we are in heaven, something or other will be in our way. Now the Spirit of God must help us over all these afflictions. We will never come to heaven without overcoming afflictions, and scandals, and the temptation with which Satan plies us here. And then to overcome the tediousness of time: this needs a great deal of strength. Now this grace of expectation does all. And therefore it is so often insisted on in Scripture. In Isaiah, and in the Psalms, how often it is repeated (Ps 37:7)! “Wait on the #smallcaps[Lord]; if He tarries, wait.” The Lord will wait for those who wait for him; and it is the mark of a Christian in Scripture. Moses speaks of such as waited for the consolation of Israel (Gen 49:18) before Christ came in the flesh; such a one is one who was “waiting for the consolation of Israel” (Luke 2:25). To have a gracious disposition and a grace of waiting was the mark of good people. Now since the coming of Christ, the mark of the New Testament is to wait for Christ’s appearing. “There is a crown of glory for me, and not only for me, but for all who love His appearing” (2 Tim 4:8). That is an ingredient in waiting, when we love the thing we wait for. And so Titus 2:12: “The grace of God that teaches us to deny ungodliness and worldly lusts, and to live holily, and justly, and soberly in this present evil world, looking for and waiting for this glorious appearing of Jesus Christ.”

So looking with the eye of the soul partly to the first coming of Christ, which was to redeem our souls, and partly to the second, which is to redeem our bodies from corruption and to make both soul and body happy, makes a man a good Christian. For the grace of God, in the first, teaches us to deny ungodliness; and looking for Christ’s appearing makes us zealous for good works. You#super[+] have scarcely any epistle but you have the time described for looking for the coming of Christ, as Jude: “Keep yourselves in the love of God, and wait for the coming of Christ.” So, as there are gracious promises, and a long day for them, God grants grace to wait for their accomplishment.

Now as God gives grace to wait, so he will perform what we wait for; as they say here, “We have waited.” That is the speech of enjoying. God will at length make good what he has promised; and what his truth has promised, his power will perform. Goodness inclines him to make a promise, truth speaks it, and power performs it, as you#super[+] will see here: “We have waited.”

In God there is a mouth of truth, a heart of pity, and a hand of power. These three meeting together make good whatever is promised. “He fulfills the desires of those who fear Him” (Ps 145:19). The desires that God has put into his children are kindled from heaven; and he will satisfy them all out of his tender pity and compassion. He will not allow the creature to be always on the rack of desire, on the rack of expectation, but he will fulfill the desire of those who fear him. And therefore learn this for the time to come.

Though we wait, God will perform whatever we wait for. And therefore, “Behold, we have waited for Him.” As there is a time of promising, so there is a time of performing; as there is a seedtime, so there is a time of harvest. There is a succession in nature, and a succession in grace: as the day follows the night, and the Sabbath the week, and the jubilee such a term of years; and as the triumph follows the war; and as the consummation of marriage follows the contract; so it is a happy and glorious condition, above all conditions here on earth. Therefore in this text you#super[+] have not only the seedtime of the Christian (we may sow in tears, and in expectation, as in sowing), but here is also the harvest of a Christian. As there is a time of sowing, so there is a time of reaping; as a time of waiting, so of enjoying. We have waited, and now, behold, we have what we waited for.

But why does the Holy Spirit not set down a fixed time, but leaves it indefinite, “In that day”? God keeps times and seasons in his own power; the time in general he leaves open. There is a day; but the point and moment of time he keeps in his own power. It is enough to know there is a day, and a day that will come in the best season. God’s time is the best time. When judgments were threatened against the wicked, they say, “Let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we die” (1 Cor 15:32). So Saul: “Tomorrow you will die” (1 Sam 28:19); and was he the better for it? So where there is a fixed time of God’s coming in judgment, godly men would be none the worse, and wicked men never the better. Therefore God keeps it indefinite, “In that day.”

There is a day, and it is a glorious day, a day of all days, a day that will never have night, a day that we should think of every day: “that day,” by way of excellence. And before that day there are particular days in this world in which God shows himself and fulfills the expectation of his children, to cherish the grand expectation of life everlasting. As in times of trouble they expect from God, and wait for deliverance in God’s time, and they must be able to say, “Behold, we have waited.” Because it is a beginning and pledge of the great performance that will be consummated at that great day, and of all the miseries that will then be removed; so there is a day when the Jews will be converted, and the fullness of the Gentiles brought in, and the man of sin revealed, and consumed by the breath of Christ. And when the church of God sees them, they may say, “Behold, we have waited for the Lord,” and behold, he has come; what we looked for is now fulfilled. So God does not reserve the fulfilling of all the promises to the great day of all days, but even in this life he will have a “that day.”

And it would be very good for Christians, in the events of their lives, to see how God answers their prayers and delivers them. Let them do as the saints in the Old Testament, who gave names to places where they saw God: as Peniel (Gen 32:30), that is, he will see God; and Abraham, “God will be seen on the mount” (Gen 22:14). So Samson and others gave names to places where they had deliverance, that they might be moved to be thankful. A Christian takes in all the comforts of this life to believe the things of the last great day. “Behold, we have waited for Him.”

That will be a time of sight and full enjoyment, of full power and full joy, which is reserved for heaven; then we will say, “Behold! See, this is the Lord.” The more we see God here, the more we will see him hereafter. There are many ways of seeing, so as to say, “Behold, this is the Lord!” We may say, from the poorest creature, “Behold, this is the Lord!” Here are beams of his majesty in the works of his justice and mercy: “Behold, here is the Lord!” The Lord has brought mighty things to pass, the Lord is marvelously loving to his children. “Behold and see the salvation of the Lord!” We may say, “Behold, here,” and see something of God in every creature. There is no creature but has something of God. The things that have only being have something of God; but the things that have life have more of God. And so in some there is more, in some less of God.

But in the church of God especially we may see his going in the sanctuary. Behold, this God has done for his church. And in the sacraments we may say, I have seen the Lord, and felt the Lord in his ordinance by his Holy Spirit. We do all this before we come to see him in heaven. But that is not what is specially meant.

We will say, “Behold, this is the Lord!” when we see him in heaven. All sight here leads to that sight. Faith has a sight here, but it is in the word and sacrament, and so imperfect; but the sight in heaven is immediate and perfect, and therefore opposed to faith. We live by faith, and not by sight. In heaven we will live by sight; not that we do not live by sight here in some degree, for the lesser sight leads to the greater sight. But in comparison with the sight in heaven, there is no sight. The Scripture speaks of the sight of God comparatively. Moses “saw God,” that is, more than any other; and Jacob “saw God,” that is, comparatively more than before, but not fully and wholly. We can apprehend him, but not comprehend him, as they say. We may see something of him, but not wholly.

But in heaven we will have another sight of God, and then we will say, “Behold, this is the God we have waited for!” We will see Christ face to face.

Beloved, that is the sight indeed. And if you#super[+] ask me whether we will see God then or not, consider what I said before. This is the God we have waited for in obedience, and fruitfully.

If we are to be enraptured with the sight of God, surely, if we see him here, we may see him there. We see him with the eye of faith, we see him in the ordinance, we have some sight of God that the world does not have. God reveals himself to his children more than to the world; and therefore they say, “You reveal Yourself to us, and not to the world” (John 14:22). A Christian wonders that God should reveal his love, and mercy, and goodness to him more than to others. And therefore, if we belong to God and will see him hereafter, we must see him now. As we may see him, we must have some knowledge of him. And if we see God in any way, all things in the world will be thought of no account in comparison with the communion of God in Christ: as, “We have seen the Lord, and what have we to do with idols?” (Hos 14:8). The soul that has seen Christ grows in detestation of sin, and loathes all things in comparison.

And then again, if we are ever to see God in glory, in this glorious and triumphing manner, “This is the #smallcaps[Lord],” this sight is a changing sight. There is no sight of God but it changes us and alters us to the likeness of God, when he calls us to look up to him, and he looks on us in favor and mercy. The best fruit of his favor is grace, peace, and joy, for these are beams that issue from him, grace, as beams from the sun. But wherever God looks with any favor, there is a conformity to Christ: a gracious, humble, compassionate, merciful, obedient disposition, which is a down payment of the Spirit of Christ. And there is a pursuit of purity, of a disposition refined from the pollutions of the world. “The pure in heart … will see God” (Matt 5:8). Those who hope to see God forever in heaven will pursue that purity that may dispose and fit them for heaven. And there is such a gracious influence in it that, for those who hope for heaven, the very hope cannot but help to purify them.

As there is grace suited to waiting, so there is an influence from the things hoped for to give vigor to all grace. As all the graces of a Christian fit and enable him for heaven, so the hope of heaven yields life to all grace. There is a mutual influence in these things. God grants a revealing of these glorious things to help us to wait, to be patient, and fruitful, and abounding in the work of the Lord. And the more we wait fruitfully, and patiently, and silently, the more we see of heaven. So as in nature the seed brings the tree, and the tree the seed, so in the things of God one thing breeds another, and that breeds that again. So waiting and grace fit us for heaven, and the thought of heaven puts life and vigor into all the graces that fit us for heaven. What is our faith, but toward those glorious things we will see hereafter? What is patience, but for the consideration of that? What is hope, but for the excellence of the object of hope? And what would the enduring of troubles be, if there were not something in heaven to make amends for all? They help us to come to glory, and the lively, hopeful thoughts of those things animate and enliven all the graces that fit us for heaven. If ever we are to possess heaven hereafter, and say, “Behold, this is he we have waited for,” we must see him here, so as to undervalue all things, to see him with a changing sight; for the object of glory cannot be revealed but it will stir up a disposition suited to glory. If this is not so, never hope for a sight of him in heaven.

And therefore let me entreat and beg you#super[+], with the apostle Paul, to “look to the end”: look to the main thing that can come in this world, and that will come hereafter. It is wisdom to look to the end. A man who builds a house will think of the end he sets before himself, that is, dwelling and habitation. We are meant for everlasting communion with God; we are to be perfect, in glory as in grace. Heaven is our element; we do not rest till then—we are in motion till then—that being our station. Then think often of this: never rest in any intermediate condition, because we are waiting till we come to that condition. Let us so carry ourselves that we may say, This we waited for; it is the glory we expected. It is our wisdom often to have the end of our lives in our eyes, that we may be helped to wait patiently, cheerfully, and comfortably till the consummation comes, when all promises will end in performance, when all that is ill and imperfectly good will be removed: a consumption of ill, and a consummation of all good.

Oh, let us have that day in our eyes, that day of all days, and the very thoughts of it will fit us for the day. The thoughts of our end will fit us and stir us up to all means tending to that end. Medicine is good if it tends to health. The very thought of that prescribes order and means. We read, “Seek first the kingdom of God … and all these things will be added unto you#super[+]” (Matt 6:33). The thought of the end prescribes order to all means, and it prescribes measure: “how to use the world as though I did not use it” (1 Cor 7:31); for the thoughts of my end stir me up to use all courses suited to that end. And therefore the best wisdom in Christians is often to set the end before them, and to be content in no grace or comfort as it is in a way of imperfection, but to look on every grace, every comfort, every good, as it tends to perfection. David did not desire to dwell in the house of God forever because he would rest his desire in the house of God here; but he aims at heaven. And so when the saints of God bound and fix their desires and contentment, it is with reference to the last day, the rest of a Christian, beyond which they cannot go, even communion with God himself.
